# Business Insights & Recommendations
## Loan Approval & Credit Risk Analysis

*Based on EDA (Tasks 3–4), visualizations (Task 5), and the Power BI dashboard (Task 6) built on 3,200 cleaned loan application records.*

---

## Key Business Insights

**1. Credit score is the strongest predictor of default risk — far more than income.**
Default rates fall steadily and consistently as credit score improves: 16.4% for "Poor" credit applicants, 13.9% for "Fair," 13.9% for "Good," dropping to 7.9% for "Very Good" and 7.1% for "Excellent." Meanwhile, the correlation between annual income and default status is weak, and the income-vs-default boxplot (Chart 5) shows heavy overlap between defaulters and non-defaulters. **Credit score, not income, should be the primary risk-weighting factor in approval decisions.**

**2. The bank is more conservative than its risk profile requires.**
Only ~41% of applications are approved overall, yet even the riskiest approved segment ("Poor" credit) defaults only 16.4% of the time — meaning over 83% of "Poor"-band borrowers who do get approved repay successfully. This suggests approval criteria may be filtering out more low-risk applicants than necessary, potentially at the cost of loan volume and interest income.

**3. Loan purpose meaningfully affects approval likelihood, independent of applicant risk.**
Business (43.3%) and Debt Consolidation (42.4%) loans have the highest approval rates, while Personal loans (37.2%) have the lowest — a 6-point gap. Since this pattern holds across the applicant pool broadly, it points to purpose-based underwriting bias rather than differences in applicant quality alone.

**4. Income and loan amount are tightly coupled (r ≈ 0.89), which concentrates risk.**
Because loan amounts are sized proportionally to income, high-income outlier applicants (up to ~₹950,000/year) also request proportionally larger loans. This means a small number of high-income defaults could have outsized financial impact compared to their share of total applications.

**5. Regional approval rates are broadly balanced, with no major geographic red flag.**
Applications are fairly evenly distributed across North (24.3%), South (19.6%), East/West/Central regions, and no single region shows a dramatically outsized default or approval rate — regional risk does not appear to be a major driver in this dataset, unlike credit score or loan purpose.

